28 Days Later (2002) is a British post-apocalyptic horror film directed by Danny Boyle.

The Outbreak:
 Animal rights activists break into a research laboratory and accidentally release chimpanzees infected with the "Rage" virus—a pathogen that instantly turns humans into vicious, bloodthirsty killers.
 
 The Awakening:
 Twenty-eight days later, a bicycle courier named Jim (Cillian Murphy) wakes up from a coma in an abandoned London hospital. He wanders through a completely deserted city before discovering that society has collapsed and the streets are overrun by fast, feral infected.
 
 The Journey:
 Jim teams up with other survivors—Selena and, later, a father and daughter named Frank and Hannah. They follow a radio broadcast claiming that military forces in Manchester have a solution and a safe haven.
 
 The Climax:
 Upon reaching the military blockade, they discover the base has fallen into ruin and the soldiers have gone rogue. Jim has to use ruthless tactics to rescue his companions from both the infected and the corrupted soldiers.
 
 The Resolution:
 The survivors eventually find refuge in a remote countryside cottage, where they wait with cautious hope as the infected outside begin to starve.
